{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg 'include\\\\(\\\"posts\\\"\\\\)|\\\\.create\\\\(' node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres -g '*.md' | head -35; rg 'User:|Post:' src/prisma/contract.d.ts | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:      A MongoDB migration creates and drops each index by its name. `prisma migration plan` writes the name into `migration.ts`, as in `createIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })` and `dropIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })`. The operation id is `index.<collection>.<create|drop>(<name>)`, such as `index.users.create(email_1)`, where it was `index.users.create(email:1)`. A `migration.ts` written by an earlier release keeps working: an index without a name is named after its keys, as before.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:A script or test that matches an operation id, such as `index.users.create(email:1)`, matches the name instead: `index.users.create(email_1)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries.md://   await db.orm.users.create(u);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:const user = await db.orm.users.create({\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:  .create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ id, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id: 1, email: 'a@x.io' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:const authUser = await db.orm.auth.User.create({ id: 2, token: 'tok' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/quickstart.md:await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/quickstart.md:> **Mongo target:** the snippet above is SQL-target shape. On `@internal/mongo`, `db.orm` is keyed by the collection's storage name (`@@map(...)`, or the lowercased model name if no `@@map`), so the same arc reads `await db.orm.users.create(...)` / `await db.orm.users.select('id', 'email').all()` — not `db.orm.public.User`. Full rule and rewrite recipe in `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing*.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:      `createMongoRuntime()` takes `{ context, adapter, driver }`, as the SQL runtime does: create the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)` and pass it beside the context. The runtime reads the target from `context.contract.target`, so build the context over the validated contract. `MongoExecutionContext` no longer has a `stack` member or a second type parameter, and its contract must be a MongoDB contract, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` requires a SQL contract: write `MongoExecutionContext<Contract>`, and build it with `createMongoExecutionContext({ contract, stack })` over a validated contract. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as for SQL. `buildMongoEnums()` is removed: read the contract's enum accessors from `db.enums`, from `mongo({ contract })` or `mongoStatic({ contract })`. `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.11-to-8.0.0-rc.12/instructions.md:const familyInstance = family.create(stack);\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgr